<p>After hail hits a Texas home, the claim follows a predictable path: you protect the property and document the damage, report the claim with a date of loss, the insurer inspects and writes an estimate, and you receive a first payment that is usually actual cash value, with more owed later if your policy pays replacement cost. Most disputes come from three places: damage the adjuster didn't count, items the estimate left out, and policy endorsements the homeowner didn't know were there.</p>
<p>This is the overview. Where a stage deserves a full article of its own, we link to it rather than repeat it.</p>

<h2>The first 72 hours after a hailstorm</h2>
<p>What you do in the first three days doesn't need to be complicated, but it should happen in this order.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Check for hazards, from the ground.</strong> Look for downed lines, broken glass, and limbs hanging over the house. Don't climb onto a wet roof that hail has just covered with loose granules and ice. A ground walk tells you most of what you need right now.</li>
<li><strong>Stop water from getting in.</strong> If the roof or a skylight is leaking, put a tarp over the opening or have a roofer do it, and catch interior water before it reaches flooring and walls. Almost every policy requires you to take reasonable steps to protect the property, and damage that spreads because nobody acted can be excluded. Keep receipts for tarps, buckets and any emergency service; those costs are normally part of the claim.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph before anything is fixed.</strong> Take wide shots of each side of the house, then close-ups of dented gutters, downspouts, window screens, the A/C condenser, fences, siding and anything else metal or painted. Inside, photograph ceiling stains and wet insulation in the attic. Temporary repairs are fine; permanent repairs before the adjuster sees the damage are not.</li>
<li><strong>Pin down the storm date.</strong> The date of loss you report should be the date hail actually fell at your address, not the day you noticed a leak. Use our <a href="/tools/storm-report-lookup/">storm report lookup</a> to find NOAA hail reports near your property and save a copy for the claim file.</li>
<li><strong>Find your policy.</strong> Pull up the declarations page and the full policy, including endorsements. You need to know your wind and hail deductible and whether the roof is covered at replacement cost or actual cash value before you talk to anyone.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our separate guide on <a href="/post/how-to-document-damage-for-your-claim/">how to document damage for your claim</a> covers photo technique and what to keep in the file.</p>

[[tool:storm-report-lookup]]

<h2>Read four things in your policy before you file</h2>
<p>Hail claims turn on policy wording more often than on the damage itself. Find these four items first.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li><strong>Wind and hail deductible.</strong> In Texas this is often a percentage of dwelling coverage, commonly 1% or 2%. On a home insured for $300,000, a 2% deductible is $6,000, so a minor claim may not clear it.</li>
<li><strong>Roof settlement terms.</strong> Some policies carry an endorsement or roof schedule that pays the roof at actual cash value only, based on its age. That changes the math more than anything an adjuster finds.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic damage exclusion.</strong> Common on metal roofs, and sometimes written for other roof types. It excludes dents and marks that don't affect how the roof works. See <a href="/post/cosmetic-vs-functional-hail-damage/">cosmetic vs. functional hail damage</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Duties after loss and suit limitation.</strong> These conditions set how quickly you must report and how long you have to sue. Our article on <a href="/post/how-long-do-you-have-to-file-a-hail-claim-in-texas/">how long you have to file a hail claim in Texas</a> explains the deadlines.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Filing the claim</h2>
<p>Report the claim by phone, app or online portal, and give the insurer the storm date, the address, and a short factual description: "Hail on May 12 dented gutters and the A/C unit; roof leaking over the back bedroom." Don't guess at the extent of roof damage or say the roof was "old anyway." Write down the claim number and the name of anyone you speak with, and follow up any phone call with a short email confirming what was said.</p>
<p>Filing starts the clock under Chapter 542 of the Texas Insurance Code. The insurer must respond within set deadlines.</p>
<div class="keyfacts">
<div><span>15 days</span><p>to acknowledge the claim, start investigating and request what it needs</p></div>
<div><span>15 business days</span><p>to accept or reject after receiving everything it asked for</p></div>
<div><span>5 business days</span><p>to pay after notifying you it accepts the claim</p></div>
<div><span>+15 days</span><p>added to these deadlines after a declared weather catastrophe</p></div>
</div>
<p>Surplus lines carriers have longer windows, and an insurer that needs more time must tell you why and then decide within 45 days of that notice. TWIA claims on the coast run on their own rules. Our <a href="/tools/texas-claim-deadline-calculator/">Texas claim deadline calculator</a> works out the dates from your own claim timeline.</p>

<h2>The adjuster inspection</h2>
<p>After a large Texas hailstorm, the adjuster who comes out is often an independent or catastrophe adjuster hired by your insurer for the event. On a typical roof inspection the adjuster will:</p>
<ul>
<li>Walk the property and look at soft metals (gutters, vents, flashing, the A/C unit) to confirm hail size and direction.</li>
<li>Mark a test square on each roof slope and count hail hits inside it.</li>
<li>Measure the roof or use aerial measurements, and note the pitch, number of layers and roof age.</li>
<li>Photograph what they find and write an estimate, usually in Xactimate or similar software.</li>
</ul>
<p>Be there for it, or have your roofer or public adjuster there. Point out every dent and leak you've found and ask which slopes the adjuster considers damaged. How carriers decide is covered in detail in <a href="/post/how-insurance-companies-evaluate-hail-damage/">how insurance companies evaluate hail damage</a>, and the signs to look for on your own roof are in our <a href="/post/how-to-identify-hail-damage-by-roof-type/">guide to hail damage by roof type</a>.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask the adjuster before they leave whether an engineer will be sent and when you'll see the estimate. Write the answer down with the date. If the timeline slips, you have a record to point to.</p></div>

<h2>How the estimate is built</h2>
<p>The insurer's estimate is a list of line items, each with a quantity, a unit price, and a depreciation amount. For a roof, the quantity is measured in squares (100 square feet each), and the estimate should list more than "remove and replace shingles." A full roof estimate includes tear-off, underlayment, starter, drip edge, ridge cap, flashing, vents and pipe jacks, a waste factor, steep or high-roof charges where they apply, and the gutters, screens and other exterior items hail damaged. Code-required items and overhead and profit belong on it when the job calls for them.</p>
<p>Read the estimate the way an accountant reads an invoice. Check the measured squares against your roofer's numbers, look for components that are missing, and note any slope the carrier left out. If the payment is short, our guide to an <a href="/post/underpaid-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">underpaid hail damage claim</a> goes through the usual gaps and how to dispute them.</p>

<h2>ACV, RCV and recoverable depreciation</h2>
<p>This is the part of a hail claim that confuses people most, and it decides how much money you see and when.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div><h3>Replacement cost value (RCV)</h3><ul><li>The cost to replace damaged property with new material of like kind and quality</li><li>Most policies pay it in two parts: ACV first, the withheld depreciation after repairs are done</li><li>The withheld amount is called recoverable depreciation</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>Actual cash value (ACV)</h3><ul><li>Replacement cost minus depreciation for age and wear</li><li>On an ACV roof endorsement, depreciation is not paid back</li><li>Disputes focus on the roof age used and the depreciation rate applied</li></ul></div>
</div>
<p>Here's a worked example on a replacement cost policy. The carrier's estimate shows $24,000 RCV for the roof and gutters, with $7,000 of depreciation, and the wind and hail deductible is $4,000.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Replacement cost (RCV)</td><td>$24,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Less depreciation withheld</td><td>−$7,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Actual cash value (ACV)</td><td>$17,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Less deductible</td><td>−$4,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>First check</td><td>$13,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recoverable depreciation, after repairs are completed</td><td>$7,000</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>To collect the second check, you generally have to complete the repairs and send proof, usually the roofer's final invoice. Your policy sets the conditions and any time limit for doing that, so read the loss settlement section before you sign a contract. Our <a href="/tools/claim-settlement-calculator/">claim settlement calculator</a> runs these numbers for your own estimate.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Watch the payee line:</strong> if you have a mortgage, the lender is usually named on the check. Call the lender's loss draft department early; they often hold funds and release them in stages as repairs progress.</p></div>

[[cta]]

<h2>Supplements: when the real job costs more</h2>
<p>Roof estimates written from the ground or from a short inspection miss things that only show up during tear-off: a second layer of shingles, rotted decking, flashing that has to be replaced, or code items the city requires for the permit. A supplement is a request to add those costs to the existing claim, supported by photos and an itemized estimate. It's routine, and insurers expect it on many roof jobs. The process, and what documentation gets one approved, is in our <a href="/post/hail-damage-supplement-claim/">hail damage supplement guide</a>.</p>
<p>A related question is whether the carrier has to pay for slopes or elevations that weren't damaged so the roof or siding matches. Texas has no statute that requires it; the answer comes from your policy wording and the facts. See <a href="/post/matching-laws-for-roof-replacement-in-texas/">matching and roof replacement in Texas</a>.</p>

<h2>When you disagree with the insurer</h2>
<p>Most hail disputes fall into one of three types, and each has a different fix.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The carrier says there's no hail damage, or that the damage is wear and tear or cosmetic.</strong> That's a coverage dispute. Get the adjuster's photos and any engineer report, have the roof independently inspected, and ask for a re-inspection with your evidence. Our guide to a <a href="/post/denied-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">denied hail damage claim</a> walks through each step.</li>
<li><strong>The carrier agrees there's damage but the number is too low.</strong> That's an amount dispute. Start with a line-by-line comparison of estimates and a written request to revise.</li>
<li><strong>Negotiation has stalled on the amount.</strong> Most Texas homeowners policies have an appraisal clause. Each side picks an appraiser, the two appraisers pick an umpire, and the panel sets the amount of loss. Appraisal doesn't decide coverage. See <a href="/post/appraisal-for-hail-damage-claim/">appraisal for hail damage claims</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a lawsuit becomes likely, Texas law requires written notice to the insurer at least 61 days before filing suit on a weather-related property claim, stating the amount you say is owed (Tex. Ins. Code §542A.003). That's the point where an attorney should be involved.</p>

<h2>How long a Texas hail claim takes</h2>
<p>A simple roof claim with a cooperative carrier can move from first report to first payment within the Chapter 542 deadlines, which on paper adds up to roughly six to seven weeks if the insurer asks for nothing extra. Real claims after a big storm run longer. Adjuster backlogs, engineer inspections, supplements, and mortgage company check handling each add weeks. A disputed claim that goes to appraisal can take several months more.</p>
<p>What you control is the speed of your own side: report promptly, answer document requests the same week, and keep every request and response in writing so any delay on the insurer's side is visible.</p>

<h2>Where a public adjuster fits</h2>
<p>A licensed public adjuster represents you, not the insurer, and works the claim from inspection through settlement. At our firm, Joseph inspects every slope and the soft metals, writes a full Xactimate estimate, submits it with photos, attends the carrier's re-inspection, and negotiates. We don't do repairs on claims we adjust, and our fee is a percentage of what the insurer pays, capped at 10% under Texas law, with no upfront charge. If your claim is straightforward and fairly paid, you may not need us, and we'll tell you so. Our <a href="/services/roof-hail-damage-claims/">roof and hail damage claims</a> page explains how we handle roof files.</p>
